// Expected: c1c0 at one session and at two.
//
// A confidentiality query follows the runtime value of a local result, which is
// the intended predicate: a session key the attacker forced really is
// disclosed. Here, though, replacing the ciphertext with an encryption of nil
// does not reveal the honest plaintext at all. It only makes Bob's local `cas_d`
// equal public nil, and the attacker then "learns" the value it supplied.
//
// The verdict stands, because Bob really does end up computing on a value the
// attacker chose. What the report must add is the distinction: the conclusion
// says the honest cas_d is not shown to be disclosed, and the headline carries
// the `attacker-supplied value` qualifier. cas_m, the thing actually worth
// protecting, holds.

attacker[active]

principal Bob[
	knows private cas_b
	cas_gb = PUBKEY(cas_b)
]

Bob -> Alice: [cas_gb]

principal Alice[
	generates cas_m
	cas_c = PKE_ENC(cas_gb, cas_m)
]

Alice -> Bob: cas_c

principal Bob[
	cas_d = PKE_DEC(cas_b, cas_c)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? cas_d
	confidentiality? cas_m
]
